Neck pain and headaches often turn up together. Sometimes the neck is genuinely contributing to the headache; sometimes they simply coexist. Understanding the difference helps you choose the right kind of care.

## A quick tour of the neck

The cervical spine is made up of seven vertebrae that support the weight of your head — around 4–6 kg — while allowing a remarkable range of movement. The upper three segments, just below the skull, share nerve pathways with parts of the head. This shared wiring is why problems in the upper neck can be felt as pain in the head.

## Common types of headache

The International Classification of Headache Disorders describes more than 200 types of headache. A few of the most relevant here:

**Tension-type headache** is the most common type. It typically feels like pressure or tightness on both sides of the head, mild to moderate, and doesn't usually worsen with routine activity. Neck and shoulder muscle tenderness is common.

**Cervicogenic headache** is headache caused by a disorder of the neck. It is usually one-sided, starts in the neck or back of the head, and may be provoked by particular neck movements or sustained positions. Neck movement is often reduced.

**Migraine** is a neurological condition with moderate to severe, often throbbing headache, frequently with nausea and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people experience aura. Neck pain is common during migraine, but the neck isn't usually the cause.

Accurate identification matters, because management differs. Migraine, for example, is usually best managed with your GP, while cervicogenic headache often responds to neck-focused exercise and manual therapy.

## Signs a headache may be neck-related

- It starts at the base of the skull or the back of the neck and spreads forward
- It's usually on the same side each time
- Certain neck positions or sustained postures bring it on
- Your neck feels stiff, and turning your head is limited
- Pressing on areas of the upper neck reproduces the familiar headache

These features suggest — but don't confirm — a neck contribution. A clinical assessment helps clarify.

## What helps with neck pain

Most neck pain is non-specific and improves over weeks. Evidence-based guidelines support:

- **Staying active** and continuing usual activities where possible
- **Exercise**, particularly strengthening the deep neck flexors and shoulder blade muscles, plus range-of-motion exercises
- **Manual therapy** — mobilisation or manipulation — combined with exercise, for some people
- **Movement variety at work.** Changing position often matters more than finding one "perfect" posture
- **Sleep and stress management**, which affect both pain and headache frequency

## What we look for

When you present with neck pain and headache, we'll take a detailed headache history — frequency, location, character, triggers and associated symptoms — and examine your neck movement, muscles and nerve function. If your headaches don't fit a musculoskeletal pattern, we'll recommend a GP review rather than treating the neck.

## When headache needs urgent medical attention

Call 000 or go to an emergency department for:

- A sudden, severe headache that peaks within a minute ("thunderclap")
- Headache with fever, a stiff neck and a rash
- Headache with confusion, weakness, numbness, vision loss or slurred speech
- Headache after a head injury, especially with vomiting or drowsiness

See your GP promptly if:

- You develop new headaches after the age of 50
- Your usual headache pattern changes significantly
- Headaches are progressively worsening
- You experience dizziness, double vision or difficulty swallowing with neck pain

## The bottom line

Some headaches are connected to the neck, and for these, a combination of exercise, manual therapy and practical changes can help. Others need a different approach. A careful assessment — and honesty about what chiropractic care can and can't offer — is the right place to start.
